<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Nyambura used the trapezoidal rule with <m>{4}</m> strips to estimate <m>\displaystyle \int_{ {0} }^{ {2} } \left({x^3+1}\right)dx</m> and got <m>{6.25}</m>.</p><p>(a) Find the exact value of the integral.</p><div>Exact value <m>=</m> <fillin characters="8" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) Find the absolute error of her estimate.</p><div>Absolute error <m>=</m> <fillin characters="8" name="ans2"/>  </div><p>(c) Find the percentage error, correct to 2 decimal places.</p><div>Percentage error <m>=</m> <fillin characters="8" name="ans3"/> %  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>The absolute error is the size of the difference between the estimate and the exact value. The percentage error compares it with the exact value: absolute error <m>\div</m> exact value <m>\times 100</m>.</p><p>(a) <m>\displaystyle \int_{ {0} }^{ {2} } \left({x^3+1}\right)dx = \left[{\frac{x^4}{4}+x}\right]_{ {0} }^{ {2} } = {6}</m>.</p><p>(b) Absolute error <m>= |{6.25} - {6}| = {0.25}</m>.</p><p>(c) Percentage error <m>= \dfrac{ {0.25} }{ {6} } \times 100 \approx {4.17}\,\%</m>.</p><p>The estimate is too big: the curve bends upwards, so the chords lie above it.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>6</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{1}{4}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{25}{6}</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>